A small IP stack for RSTS/E V10.1 that runs as an ordinary detached job,
TCPIPD, on the RSTS/E Ethernet portal interface (XE:, DEUNA/DELUA). No
monitor changes.

  - ARP (with a cache; requests for the next hop), ping replies
  - PING command: PING a.b.c.d [count]  - replies with times, or "host
    unreachable" / "network unreachable"; no privilege needed
  - Routing by plain CIDR: hosts on our network (dst & mask) are reached
    directly, anything else through the gateway (e.g. a /23 works)
  - DHCP client: address, mask, router and lease from your network; the
    host name sent is HOSTNAME, else the DECnet node name
  - TFTP server (RFC 1350): read and write, files in FTP$:; TFTP NO in
    TCPIP.INI turns it off. Exact lengths: a file written over TFTP gets
    RMS attributes recording its true length, and a file with attributes
    (TKB output, earlier TFTP writes) is sent up to that length
  - Coexists with DECnet/E: start DECnet first and give its node address
    (DECNET area.node name) in TCPIP$:TCPIP.INI

Programs talk to TCPIPD with RSTS local messages (receiver "TCPIP "); see
TCPMSG.H. PING is the first such client.

Files (TCPIP$: = SY:[0,91]):
  TCPIPD.TSK  the TCP/IP job     PING.TSK   the ping client
  TCPIP.INI   settings           TCPIP.INS  start-up file (also defines PING)
  *.C *.H *.MAC  sources

Start at boot: add  $ @SY:[0,91]TCPIP.INS  to [0,1]START.COM.
TFTP use: tftp <address>, then get/put; file names are plain RSTS names
(e.g. HELLO.C). A file without RMS attributes (made by CREATE, an editor or
C stdio) has no recorded length: netascii drops the NUL padding of its last
block, binary (octet) sends whole 512-byte blocks.

Changes since V0.1: TFTPD is now TCPIPD, the general TCP/IP job; ARP
requests and cache; CIDR next hop; local message receiver and the PING
command; TFTP optional; TFTP keeps exact file lengths, can read files that
carry RMS attributes (such as .TSK files) and replaces existing files.

Known limits: one TFTP transfer at a time; no IP fragments; no TFTP
options (RFC 2347); netascii is sent as stored (CR LF). No TCP yet.
